remaining silent outside his home this  morning prince charles's right-hand man  michael fawcett embroiled in yet another  scandal this time the allegation is hard  to deny a letter with his signature  leaked to the sunday newspapers states  in black and white that one of the  prince's charities is happy and willing  to support an application for  citizenship and that they will apply for  an honorary cbe to be made a knighthood  the recipient of this letter a saudi  billionaire who donated tens of  thousands of pounds to organizations  linked to the prince  mr fawcett has strongly denied any  wrongdoing but has temporarily stepped  down as chief executive of the prince's  foundation in a statement the foundation  said  michael fawcett offered to step down  temporarily from active duties as chief  executive of the prince's foundation  while the trustees investigation is  ongoing  the prince's foundation has accepted  this offer michael fully supports the  ongoing investigation and has confirmed  that he will assist the investigation in  every way  but some are calling for action to go  further what i'm calling for first of  all is not for the princess foundation  to investigate it because they are  compromised  that's judging during your own court the  serious allegation which is that an  honour was offered for sale needs to be  investigated by the police because it's  a criminal offence if proven under the  1925 honours act  beyond that the whole issue of how  prince charles raises money needs to be  properly investigated a palace source  has told us that clarence house will not  be commenting and rather they say this  is a matter for the prince's foundation  they have confirmed though that prince  charles has been made aware of the  allegations and while they say that the  prince and mr fawcett are not friends  there's no denying that this is a man  the prince trusted one who he worked  very closely with and a man who's been  by his side for decades  michael fawcett was just 17 when he  started his royal career as a footman to  the queen he rose the ranks quickly to  become prince charles's valet and one of  his closest advisors despite a series of  controversies including bullying  allegations in 1998 and alleged racist  comments he was repeatedly brought back  into the royal fold in 2003 he resigned  for the second time after an inquiry  into selling royal gifts but he  continued to work with prince charles  first as a freelance party planner and  then leading one of the prince's pet  projects the renovation of dumfries  house before being appointed chief  executive of the prince's foundation in  2018.  these allegations of cash for honours  are hugely embarrassing for the prince  but are ones he can't easily hide from  instead he's under pressure too there  are questions over how much he knew  about what was going on at his own  charities and of undue influence over  the honor system it could be a long wait  though for any answers charlotte lomas  sky news
 

